§ `NOT reaching over a same-line connective is refused (SET-OPERATORS-SPEC §18.1, R-NOT-1)`

-- On one line NOT takes everything after it, so each rule below reads as
-- NOT (… AND …) — the opposite of what the words say to most readers. The
-- checker refuses the spelling rather than returning the wrong BOOLEAN; the
-- golden pins the message, which shows both readings spelled out. The
-- NOT-REACH-OK markers are for the text checker (etc/check-not-precedence.mjs),
-- which sweeps this directory too and would otherwise report these on purpose.

-- the plain form, once per connective
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
notAnd a b MEANS NOT a AND b -- NOT-REACH-OK: the refused spelling, on purpose

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
notOr a b MEANS NOT a OR b -- NOT-REACH-OK

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
notImplies a b MEANS NOT a IMPLIES b -- NOT-REACH-OK

-- NOT in the middle of a line: it still reaches to the end, so this is
-- a AND (NOT (b AND c)), not a AND (NOT b) AND c
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
middle a b c MEANS a AND NOT b AND c -- NOT-REACH-OK

-- a connective on the NOT's line and another on the next: refused for the
-- AND, which is the one that shares the NOT's line
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
mixed a b c MEANS
  NOT a AND b -- NOT-REACH-OK
      OR c

-- in a directive, the same
#EVAL NOT TRUE AND FALSE -- NOT-REACH-OK
